+/*
+ * IMAGE 227: BRUTE-FORCE DIAGNOSTIC. Force the GX leaf clock gate.
+ *
+ * gpu_cc_gx_gmu_clk is a plain software gate at GPU_CC + 0x162, mask 0x0F
+ * (drivers/clk/qcom/debugcc-sm8150.c:785:
+ *    { "gpu_cc_gx_gmu_clk", 0x162, 1, GPU_CC, 0xF, 0xFF, ... })
+ *
+ * Its parent chain is already fully enabled (gpll0 en=2 600MHz ->
+ * gpll0_out_even en=2 300MHz -> gcc_gpu_gpll0_div_clk_src en=1), so the leaf
+ * bit is the only thing missing. Image 226 proved this is NOT the GX GDSC
+ * regulator: regulator_enable(gmu->gx) returned 0 and the gate stayed shut.
+ *
+ * This asks the one question that separates the two remaining worlds:
+ *
+ *   gate forced ON + fence completes  -> the firmware simply never opens it.
+ *                                     The fault is the GMU->AOP path and the
+ *                                     fix is the AOP/QMP ACD notification.
+ *   gate forced ON + still no fence  -> the break is deeper than power at all
+ *                                     (RB init, UBWC, address mode), and the
+ *                                     power story was a red herring.
+ *
+ * We write the register directly because clock_gpucc exposes no clock-names,
+ * so the clock cannot be requested through the clk framework. That bypasses
+ * refcounting, which is acceptable for a one-shot experiment but is NOT a fix.
+ */
